    body_en: "The word classic does not mean old; it means having survived across repeated eras. The process by which a work rises into the ranks of the classics is never natural, and behind it operate the tastes and institutional choices of a particular generation. Only when university curricula, publishers' decisions to reissue a book, and critics' repeated mentions come together does a work finally land on the list of must-reads. The fact that works by women writers or non-Western writers have long been excluded from this process shows that the canon's list is itself a product of power. Even so, rereading the classics has a value of its own that goes beyond the politics of the list. Old texts reveal that emotions and institutions we now take for granted are in fact historically constructed. For instance, one can confirm through classic novels that the notion of romantic love was formed in a particular era. The classics also possess the peculiar property of revealing a different meaning with each repeated reading. If what one feels reading the same work now differs from what one felt ten years ago, that is not because the text changed but because the reader did. In this respect, reading the classics functions as a mirror for self-understanding. An attitude that critically reexamines the canon's list while not denying the power held by the individual works within it — this would be a mature way of approaching the classics today.",
